/-!
# Computing an intersection multiplicity from a regular sequence

Let `A` be a regular local ring, let `I` be an ideal generated by a regular sequence
`f₁, …, f_c`, and let `J` be a second ideal. Assume that `A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)` is zero dimensional and
that `dim A ≤ dim (A ⧸ I) + dim (A ⧸ J)`. Then the function

`t ↦ length_A ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ (f₁, …, f_c) ^ t (A ⧸ J))`

agrees for `t ≫ 0` with a rational polynomial of degree exactly `c` whose leading coefficient is
positive, and the intersection multiplicity of the pair computed by the Tor formula is `c !`
times that leading coefficient.

Geometrically, for `A` the local ring of a nonsingular variety `X` at the generic point `ξ` of an
irreducible component `Z` of `V ∩ W`, with `I` the local ideal of a closed subvariety `V` cut out
at `ξ` by the regular sequence and `J` the local ideal of a closed subvariety `W`, the hypothesis
`dim A ≤ dim (A ⧸ I) + dim (A ⧸ J)` says that `V` and `W` intersect properly along `Z` and the
vanishing of `dim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J))` says that `Z` is an irreducible component of `V ∩ W`. The
conclusion is then `e(X, V · W, Z) = c !` times the leading coefficient of the Hilbert polynomial
`t ↦ length_{𝒪_{X, ξ}} 𝒪_{W, ξ} / (f₁, …, f_c) ^ t`, which is positive.

When `c = 1`, so that `V` is an effective Cartier divisor, and `A ⧸ J` is a domain, the Hilbert
polynomial is linear and the formula collapses to the single length
`length_A ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ f₁ (A ⧸ J))`.

## Main definitions

No new definitions are introduced.

## Main results

* `Ideal.length_tor_quotient_span_eq_zero_of_lt`: the Tor modules against the quotient by a
  regular sequence of length `c` vanish above degree `c`.
* `Ideal.localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_generalizedHilbertSamuelMultiplicity`: the Tor-formula
  multiplicity of such a pair is the generalized Hilbert–Samuel multiplicity `e_I(M, c)`.
* `Ideal.exists_computesGeneralizedHilbertPoly` and
  `Ideal.degree_eq_supportDim_of_computesGeneralizedHilbertPoly`: the generalized Hilbert function
  of a finite module for an ideal with zero-dimensional quotient is computed by a rational
  polynomial, whose degree is the dimension of the support of the module.
* `Stacks.Intersection.ringKrullDim_quotient_eq_of_intersectProperly`: the dimension of `A ⧸ J` is
  the length `c` of the regular sequence.
* `Stacks.Intersection.localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_factorial_mul_leadingCoeff`: the
  multiplicity formula together with the positivity of the leading coefficient.
* `Stacks.Intersection.localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_zero_of_isRegularSequence`: the
  complementary case, in which `dim (A ⧸ J)` falls short of `c` and the multiplicity vanishes.
* `Stacks.Intersection.localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_length_quotient_smul_of_isDomain`: the
  same multiplicity for `c = 1` and `A ⧸ J` a domain, as the length of `(A ⧸ J) ⧸ f₁ (A ⧸ J)`.

## Implementation notes

The source states the lemma for a nonsingular variety `X`, closed subvarieties `V`, `W`
intersecting properly and an irreducible component `Z` of `V ∩ W`; as for the multiplicity-one
case `Ideal.localIntersectionMultiplicity` already in the library, every hypothesis is
transported to the local ring `A = 𝒪_{X, ξ}` at the generic point `ξ` of `Z`, where the
multiplicity is defined and where the whole argument takes place. Writing `r = dim V`,
`s = dim W`, `n = dim X` and `z = dim Z`, the local dimension formula on `X`, `V` and `W` turns
`dim Z ≤ r + s - n` into `n - z ≤ (r - z) + (s - z)`, which is the hypothesis
`dim A ≤ dim (A ⧸ I) + dim (A ⧸ J)`.

The dimension count replaces the source's: that `dim (A ⧸ J) = c` is at most `c` by Krull's
height theorem, because the maximal ideal is minimal over the `c`-generated ideal
`I (A ⧸ J)`, and at least `c` because a regular sequence of length `c` drops the dimension by
exactly `c`, so `dim (A ⧸ I) + c = dim A ≤ dim (A ⧸ I) + dim (A ⧸ J)`. Only the inequality
`dim Z ≤ r + s - n` recorded by proper intersection is used; the reverse inequality for a
component of a proper intersection is not needed.

Degrees of Hilbert polynomials are available in the library for an ideal of definition of the
ring, so the Hilbert function of `M` for `I` is compared with the one of `M` over
`A ⧸ Ann(M)`, where the image of `I` is an ideal of definition. The unshifted (generalized)
Hilbert function of the source and the shifted Hilbert–Samuel function of the library differ by
composing the computing polynomial with `X ± 1`, which does not change its degree.

Beyond the Noetherian local hypotheses, regularity of `A` is not used: it is kept because the
source assumes `X` nonsingular, and the auxiliary results are stated without it. The same holds
for the case `c = 1`.

For `c = 1` the source's standing assumption that `W` is a closed subvariety is used, in the form
that `A ⧸ J` is a domain: the vanishing of `dim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J))` makes `f₁ ∉ J`, and in a domain
that is enough for `f₁` to be a nonzerodivisor on `A ⧸ J`, whence
`length_A ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ f₁ⁿ (A ⧸ J)) = n · length_A ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ f₁ (A ⧸ J))`. The hypothesis cannot
be dropped: for `A = k[x, y]` localized at the origin, `I = (y)` and `J = (x², xy)`, all the
hypotheses of Tag 0B04 hold with `c = 1`, the Hilbert polynomial is `t + 1` so the multiplicity
is `1`, while `length_A ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ f₁ (A ⧸ J)) = 2`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B04](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B04)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B05](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B05)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B03](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B03)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 02MC](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02MC)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0AZR](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AZR)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Filter Polynomial
open scoped ExteriorAlgebra TensorProduct

universe u

namespace Ideal

/-! ### Shifting between the Hilbert–Samuel and the generalized Hilbert function -/

private lemma degree_comp_X_add_one (P : ℚ[X]) : (P.comp (X + 1)).degree = P.degree := by
  rcases eq_or_ne P 0 with rfl | hP0
  · simp
  · have hback : (P.comp (X + 1)).comp (X - 1) = P := by
      rw [Polynomial.comp_assoc, show ((X : ℚ[X]) + 1).comp (X - 1) = X by simp,
        Polynomial.comp_X]
    have hne : P.comp (X + 1) ≠ 0 := fun h => hP0 (by rw [← hback, h, Polynomial.zero_comp])
    rw [Polynomial.degree_eq_natDegree hne, Polynomial.degree_eq_natDegree hP0,
      Polynomial.natDegree_comp]
    simp

/-- A polynomial computing the generalized Hilbert function, precomposed with `X + 1`, computes
the Hilbert–Samuel function. -/
theorem computesHilbertSamuelPoly_comp_X_add_one {R M : Type*} [CommRing R]
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] (I : Ideal R) {P : ℚ[X]}
    (hP : I.ComputesGeneralizedHilbertPoly M P) :
    I.ComputesHilbertSamuelPoly M (P.comp (X + 1)) := by
  filter_upwards [(Filter.tendsto_add_atTop_nat 1).eventually hP] with n hn
  obtain ⟨p, hp1, hp2⟩ := hn
  exact ⟨p, hp1, by rw [Polynomial.eval_comp]; simpa using hp2⟩

/-- A polynomial computing the Hilbert–Samuel function, precomposed with `X - 1`, computes the
generalized Hilbert function. -/
theorem computesGeneralizedHilbertPoly_comp_X_sub_one {R M : Type*} [CommRing R]
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] (I : Ideal R) {Q : ℚ[X]}
    (hQ : I.ComputesHilbertSamuelPoly M Q) :
    I.ComputesGeneralizedHilbertPoly M (Q.comp (X - 1)) := by
  obtain ⟨N, hN⟩ := Filter.eventually_atTop.mp hQ
  refine Filter.eventually_atTop.mpr ⟨N + 1, fun n hn => ?_⟩
  obtain ⟨k, rfl⟩ : ∃ k, n = k + 1 := ⟨n - 1, by omega⟩
  obtain ⟨p, hp1, hp2⟩ := hN k (by omega)
  exact ⟨p, hp1, by rw [Polynomial.eval_comp]; push_cast; simpa using hp2⟩

/-! ### The degree of the Hilbert polynomial of a module -/

/-- The degree of a polynomial computing the Hilbert–Samuel function of `N` for an ideal of
definition of the ring is the dimension of the support of `N`: the degree does not depend on the
ideal of definition, so it may be compared with the one for the maximal ideal. -/
private lemma degree_map_eq_supportDim_of_radical_eq
    {R N : Type*} [CommRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R] [IsLocalRing R]
    [AddCommGroup N] [Module R N] [Module.Finite R N]
    {I : Ideal R} (hI : I.radical = IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R) {P : ℚ[X]}
    (hP : I.ComputesHilbertSamuelPoly N P) :
    P.degree.map (Nat.cast : ℕ → ℕ∞) = Module.supportDim R N := by
  obtain ⟨Q, hQ⟩ := Ideal.exists_computesHilbertSamuelPoly (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R)
    ((IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal.isMaximal R).isPrime.radical) N
  obtain ⟨_de, e, he⟩ :=
    Ideal.exists_degree_eq_of_eventually_eval_eq_length_quotient_smul_pow (R := R) N
  have hPI : P.degree = e := (he I hI P).2 hP
  have hQm : Q.degree = e := (he (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R)
    ((IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal.isMaximal R).isPrime.radical) Q).2 hQ
  rw [hPI, ← hQm, ← Ideal.hilbertSamuelDegree_eq_degree_of_computes hQ]
  exact Module.hilbertSamuelDegree_eq_supportDim R N

/-- For a finite module `M` over a Noetherian local ring with `M ⧸ I M` of dimension zero, the
Hilbert–Samuel function of `M` for `I` is computed by a rational polynomial, and the degree of
any such polynomial is the dimension of the support of `M`. -/
@[ega "0 16.2.3" "special case"]
theorem exists_and_degree_computesHilbertSamuelPoly
    {A M : Type u} [CommRing A] [IsNoetherianRing A] [IsLocalRing A]
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M] [Module.Finite A M] (I : Ideal A)
    (h0 : Module.supportDim A (M ⧸ I • (⊤ : Submodule A M)) = 0) :
    (∃ Q : ℚ[X], I.ComputesHilbertSamuelPoly M Q) ∧
      ∀ Q : ℚ[X], I.ComputesHilbertSamuelPoly M Q →
        Q.degree.map (Nat.cast : ℕ → ℕ∞) = Module.supportDim A M := by
  have hne : Nontrivial (M ⧸ I • (⊤ : Submodule A M)) :=
    (Module.supportDim_ne_bot_iff_nontrivial A _).mp (by rw [h0]; norm_num)
  have : Nontrivial M := (I • (⊤ : Submodule A M)).mkQ_surjective.nontrivial
  let K : Ideal A := Module.annihilator A M
  let B : Type u := A ⧸ K
  let : Module B M := Module.quotientAnnihilator
  have hBnt : Nontrivial B := Ideal.Quotient.nontrivial_iff.mpr
    (mt Module.annihilator_eq_top_iff.mp (not_subsingleton M))
  have : IsLocalRing B :=
    IsLocalRing.of_surjective' (Ideal.Quotient.mk K) Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective
  have : Module.Finite B M := Module.Finite.of_restrictScalars_finite A B M
  have hann : Module.annihilator B M = ⊥ := by
    refine le_antisymm (fun b hb => ?_) bot_le
    obtain ⟨a, rfl⟩ := Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective b
    exact Ideal.Quotient.eq_zero_iff_mem.mpr
      (Module.mem_annihilator.mpr fun x => Module.mem_annihilator.mp hb x)
  have hsd : Module.supportDim B M = Module.supportDim A M := by
    rw [Module.supportDim_eq_ringKrullDim_quotient_annihilator (R := B),
      Module.supportDim_eq_ringKrullDim_quotient_annihilator (R := A), hann]
    exact ringKrullDim_eq_of_ringEquiv (RingEquiv.quotientBot B)
  set J : Ideal B := Ideal.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk K) I with hJdef
  have hdef : IsIdealOfDefinition J :=
    ((Ideal.idealOfDefinition_support_length_tfae I hne).out 4 2).mp (le_of_eq h0)
  have hlen : ∀ n : ℕ, Module.length A (M ⧸ (I ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule A M))) =
      Module.length B (M ⧸ (J ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule B M))) := by
    intro n
    have hp := Ideal.map_quotientAnnihilator_pow_smul_top (R := A) (M := M) I n
    calc
      Module.length A (M ⧸ (I ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule A M))) =
          Module.length A (M ⧸ (J ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule B M)).restrictScalars A) :=
        (Submodule.quotEquivOfEq _ _ hp.symm).length_eq
      _ = Module.length B (M ⧸ (J ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule B M))) :=
        length_restrictScalars_eq_of_surjective A B (Ideal.Quotient.mk K)
          Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective (M ⧸ (J ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule B M)))
  have htransfer : ∀ Q : ℚ[X], I.ComputesHilbertSamuelPoly M Q ↔
      J.ComputesHilbertSamuelPoly M Q := by
    refine fun Q => ⟨fun hQ => ?_, fun hQ => ?_⟩
    · filter_upwards [hQ] with n hn
      obtain ⟨p, hp1, hp2⟩ := hn
      exact ⟨p, by rwa [← hlen (n + 1)], hp2⟩
    · filter_upwards [hQ] with n hn
      obtain ⟨p, hp1, hp2⟩ := hn
      exact ⟨p, by rwa [hlen (n + 1)], hp2⟩
  refine ⟨?_, fun Q hQ => ?_⟩
  · obtain ⟨Q, hQ⟩ := Ideal.exists_computesHilbertSamuelPoly J hdef M
    exact ⟨Q, (htransfer Q).mpr hQ⟩
  · rw [← hsd]
    exact degree_map_eq_supportDim_of_radical_eq hdef ((htransfer Q).mp hQ)

/-- For a finite module `M` over a Noetherian local ring with `M ⧸ I M` of dimension zero, the
generalized Hilbert function of `M` for `I` is computed by a rational polynomial. -/
theorem exists_computesGeneralizedHilbertPoly
    {A M : Type u} [CommRing A] [IsNoetherianRing A] [IsLocalRing A]
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M] [Module.Finite A M] (I : Ideal A)
    (h0 : Module.supportDim A (M ⧸ I • (⊤ : Submodule A M)) = 0) :
    ∃ P : ℚ[X], I.ComputesGeneralizedHilbertPoly M P := by
  obtain ⟨Q, hQ⟩ := (exists_and_degree_computesHilbertSamuelPoly I h0).1
  exact ⟨Q.comp (X - 1), computesGeneralizedHilbertPoly_comp_X_sub_one I hQ⟩

/-- The degree of a polynomial computing the generalized Hilbert function of `M` for `I` is the
dimension of the support of `M`, provided `M ⧸ I M` has dimension zero. -/
theorem degree_eq_supportDim_of_computesGeneralizedHilbertPoly
    {A M : Type u} [CommRing A] [IsNoetherianRing A] [IsLocalRing A]
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M] [Module.Finite A M] (I : Ideal A)
    (h0 : Module.supportDim A (M ⧸ I • (⊤ : Submodule A M)) = 0)
    {P : ℚ[X]} (hP : I.ComputesGeneralizedHilbertPoly M P) :
    P.degree.map (Nat.cast : ℕ → ℕ∞) = Module.supportDim A M := by
  have h := (exists_and_degree_computesHilbertSamuelPoly I h0).2 _
    (computesHilbertSamuelPoly_comp_X_add_one I hP)
  rwa [degree_comp_X_add_one] at h

/-- The generalized Hilbert–Samuel multiplicity in degree `d` is `d !` times the coefficient of
degree `d` of any polynomial computing the generalized Hilbert function. -/
theorem generalizedHilbertSamuelMultiplicity_eq_factorial_mul_coeff {R : Type*} [CommRing R]
    (I : Ideal R) (M : Type*) [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] {P : ℚ[X]}
    (hP : I.ComputesGeneralizedHilbertPoly M P) (d : ℕ) :
    I.generalizedHilbertSamuelMultiplicity M d = (d.factorial : ℚ) * P.coeff d := by
  classical
  have hex : ∃ Q : ℚ[X], I.ComputesGeneralizedHilbertPoly M Q := ⟨P, hP⟩
  rw [Ideal.generalizedHilbertSamuelMultiplicity, dite_eq_left hex]
  congr 2
  exact Ideal.ComputesGeneralizedHilbertPoly.unique hex.choose_spec hP

/-! ### Vanishing of the Tor modules above the length of the sequence -/

/-- **The Tor modules against the quotient by a regular sequence of length `r` vanish above
degree `r`**: the Koszul complex computing them is concentrated in degrees `0, …, r`. -/
theorem length_tor_quotient_span_eq_zero_of_lt {A : Type u} [CommRing A] {r : ℕ} (f : Fin r → A)
    (hf : RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular A (List.ofFn f)) (I : Ideal A)
    (hI : I = Ideal.span (Set.range f)) (M : Type u) [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M]
    {i : ℕ} (hi : r < i) :
    Module.length A (((Tor (ModuleCat.{u} A) i).obj (ModuleCat.of A (A ⧸ I))).obj
      (ModuleCat.of A M)) = 0 := by
  have hzero : IsZero ((Koszul.seqComplexTensor f M).homology i) := by
    rw [← HomologicalComplex.exactAt_iff_isZero_homology]
    exact HomologicalComplex.ExactAt.of_isZero
      (ModuleCat.isZero_iff_subsingleton.mpr (koszulTensor_subsingleton_above f M i hi))
  rw [(Koszul.torIsoHomologySeqComplexTensorOfIsRegular f hf I hI M i).toLinearEquiv.length_eq]
  exact Module.length_eq_zero_iff.mpr (ModuleCat.isZero_iff_subsingleton.mp hzero)

/-- For an ideal generated by a regular sequence of length `r`, the unbounded alternating sum
defining the Tor-formula multiplicity is the finite alternating sum over `0, …, r`. -/
theorem localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_sum_tor_length {A : Type u} [CommRing A] {r : ℕ}
    (f : Fin r → A) (hf : RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular A (List.ofFn f)) (I : Ideal A)
    (hI : I = Ideal.span (Set.range f)) (M : Type u) [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M] :
    I.localIntersectionMultiplicity M =
      ∑ i : Fin (r + 1), (-1 : ℚ) ^ (i : ℕ) *
        ((Module.length A (((Tor (ModuleCat.{u} A) (i : ℕ)).obj (ModuleCat.of A (A ⧸ I))).obj
          (ModuleCat.of A M))).toNat : ℚ) := by
  rw [Ideal.localIntersectionMultiplicity,
    Fin.sum_univ_eq_sum_range (fun i : ℕ => (-1 : ℚ) ^ i *
      ((Module.length A (((Tor (ModuleCat.{u} A) i).obj (ModuleCat.of A (A ⧸ I))).obj
        (ModuleCat.of A M))).toNat : ℚ)) (r + 1)]
  refine tsum_eq_sum fun b hb => ?_
  rw [Finset.mem_range, not_lt] at hb
  rw [length_tor_quotient_span_eq_zero_of_lt f hf I hI M (by omega)]
  simp

/-- **The Tor-formula multiplicity of an ideal generated by a regular sequence is its generalized
Hilbert–Samuel multiplicity.** This is Tag 0B03 together with the vanishing of the Tor modules
above the length of the sequence. -/
theorem localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_generalizedHilbertSamuelMultiplicity
    {A : Type u} [CommRing A] [IsNoetherianRing A] [IsLocalRing A] {r : ℕ} (f : Fin r → A)
    (I : Ideal A) (M : Type u) [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M] [Module.Finite A M]
    (hf : RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular A (List.ofFn f)) (hI : I = Ideal.span (Set.range f))
    (hM : Module.supportDim A (M ⧸ I • (⊤ : Submodule A M)) = 0) :
    I.localIntersectionMultiplicity M = I.generalizedHilbertSamuelMultiplicity M r := by
  rw [localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_sum_tor_length f hf I hI M,
    Ideal.generalizedHilbertSamuelMultiplicity_eq_alternatingSum_tor_length I r f M hf hI hM]

/-! ### The generalized Hilbert function of a principal ideal generated by a regular element -/

open scoped Pointwise in
/-- For an ideal generated by a single element `a` acting injectively on `M` with `M ⧸ a M` of
finite length, the generalized Hilbert function of `M` is linear: `length_A (M ⧸ aⁿ M)` is
`n · length_A (M ⧸ a M)`, so it is computed by the polynomial `length_A (M ⧸ a M) · X`. -/
private lemma computesGeneralizedHilbertPoly_of_eq_span_singleton
    {A M : Type u} [CommRing A] [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M] {a : A} (I : Ideal A)
    (hIa : I = Ideal.span {a}) (ha : IsSMulRegular M a)
    (hfin : Module.length A (M ⧸ I • (⊤ : Submodule A M)) ≠ ⊤) :
    I.ComputesGeneralizedHilbertPoly M
      (C ((Module.length A (M ⧸ I • (⊤ : Submodule A M))).toNat : ℚ) * X) := by
  have hsm : I • (⊤ : Submodule A M) = a • (⊤ : Submodule A M) := by
    rw [hIa]
    exact Submodule.ideal_span_singleton_smul a ⊤
  have hLQ : Module.length A (M ⧸ I • (⊤ : Submodule A M))
      = Module.length A (QuotSMulTop a M) := (Submodule.quotEquivOfEq _ _ hsm).length_eq
  have hfin' : Module.length A (QuotSMulTop a M) ≠ ⊤ := hLQ ▸ hfin
  have hpow : ∀ n : ℕ, Module.length A (QuotSMulTop (a ^ n) M)
      = (n : ℕ∞) * Module.length A (QuotSMulTop a M) := by
    intro n
    induction n with
    | zero => simp [QuotSMulTop]
    | succ n ih =>
      rw [pow_succ, Module.length_quotSMulTop_mul ha, ih]
      push_cast
      ring
  rw [hLQ, hIa]
  filter_upwards with n
  refine ⟨n * (Module.length A (QuotSMulTop a M)).toNat, ?_, ?_⟩
  · have hsub : (Ideal.span {a}) ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule A M) = (a ^ n) • (⊤ : Submodule A M) := by
      rw [Ideal.span_singleton_pow]
      exact Submodule.ideal_span_singleton_smul (a ^ n) (⊤ : Submodule A M)
    rw [hsub, hpow n, Nat.cast_mul, ENat.natCast_toNat hfin']
  · simp [Nat.cast_mul, mul_comm]

end Ideal

namespace Stacks.Intersection

/-! ### The dimension of the local ring of the second subvariety -/

private alias exists_nat_ringKrullDim :=
  Stacks.RegularLocalRing.KernelRegularity.exists_natCast_eq_ringKrullDim

/-- If `A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)` is zero dimensional then `I ⊔ J` is a proper ideal. -/
private lemma sup_ne_top_of_ringKrullDim_quotient_eq_zero {A : Type u} [CommRing A]
    [IsNoetherianRing A] (I J : Ideal A) (h0 : ringKrullDim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0) : I ⊔ J ≠ ⊤ := by
  refine Ideal.Quotient.nontrivial_iff.mp ((Module.supportDim_ne_bot_iff_nontrivial A _).mp ?_)
  rw [Module.supportDim_quotient_eq_ringKrullDim, h0]
  norm_num

/-- **Krull's height theorem at the generic point.** If `A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)` is zero dimensional and `I`
is generated by `c` elements, then `A ⧸ J` has dimension at most `c`. -/
theorem ringKrullDim_quotient_le_of_ringKrullDim_quotient_sup_eq_zero
    {A : Type u} [CommRing A] [IsNoetherianRing A] [IsLocalRing A]
    {c : ℕ} (f : Fin c → A) (I J : Ideal A)
    (hI : I = Ideal.span (Set.range f))
    (h0 : ringKrullDim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0) :
    ringKrullDim (A ⧸ J) ≤ (c : WithBot ℕ∞) := by
  classical
  have hIJtop := sup_ne_top_of_ringKrullDim_quotient_eq_zero I J h0
  have hJtop : J ≠ ⊤ := fun h => hIJtop (top_le_iff.mp (h ▸ le_sup_right))
  have : Nontrivial (A ⧸ J) := Ideal.Quotient.nontrivial_iff.mpr hJtop
  have : IsLocalRing (A ⧸ J) :=
    IsLocalRing.of_surjective' (Ideal.Quotient.mk J) Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective
  set s : Finset (A ⧸ J) := Finset.image (fun i => Ideal.Quotient.mk J (f i)) Finset.univ with hs
  have hmapne : Ideal.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk J) I ≠ ⊤ := by
    intro h
    have hc := Ideal.comap_map_of_surjective (Ideal.Quotient.mk J)
      Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective I
    have hker : Ideal.comap (Ideal.Quotient.mk J) (⊥ : Ideal (A ⧸ J)) = J := Ideal.mk_ker
    rw [h, Ideal.comap_top, hker] at hc
    exact hIJtop hc.symm
  have hspan : Ideal.span (↑s : Set (A ⧸ J)) = Ideal.map (Ideal.Quotient.mk J) I := by
    rw [hI, Ideal.map_span, hs]
    congr 1
    simp only [Finset.coe_image, Finset.coe_univ, Set.image_univ]
    exact Set.range_comp _ f
  have hsub : (↑s : Set (A ⧸ J)) ⊆ (Ring.jacobson (A ⧸ J) : Set (A ⧸ J)) := by
    rw [IsLocalRing.ringJacobson_eq_maximalIdeal]
    intro x hx
    exact IsLocalRing.le_maximalIdeal hmapne (hspan ▸ Ideal.subset_span hx)
  have hdim0 : ringKrullDim ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ Ideal.span (↑s : Set (A ⧸ J))) = 0 := by
    rw [hspan, ringKrullDim_eq_of_ringEquiv (DoubleQuot.quotQuotEquivQuotSup J I), sup_comm]
    exact h0
  calc ringKrullDim (A ⧸ J)
      ≤ ringKrullDim ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ Ideal.span (↑s : Set (A ⧸ J))) + (s.card : WithBot ℕ∞) :=
        ringKrullDim_le_ringKrullDim_quotient_add_card s hsub
    _ = (s.card : WithBot ℕ∞) := by rw [hdim0, zero_add]
    _ ≤ (c : WithBot ℕ∞) := by
      have hc : s.card ≤ c := Finset.card_image_le.trans (by simp)
      exact_mod_cast hc

/-- **The dimension of `𝒪_{W, ξ}` is the length of the regular sequence cutting out `V`.** If `I`
is generated by a regular sequence of length `c`, the quotient `A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)` is zero dimensional
and `dim A ≤ dim (A ⧸ I) + dim (A ⧸ J)`, then `dim (A ⧸ J) = c`. -/
theorem ringKrullDim_quotient_eq_of_intersectProperly
    {A : Type u} [CommRing A] [IsNoetherianRing A] [IsLocalRing A]
    {c : ℕ} (f : Fin c → A) (I J : Ideal A)
    (hf : RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular A (List.ofFn f))
    (hI : I = Ideal.span (Set.range f))
    (h0 : ringKrullDim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0)
    (hproper : ringKrullDim A ≤ ringKrullDim (A ⧸ I) + ringKrullDim (A ⧸ J)) :
    ringKrullDim (A ⧸ J) = (c : WithBot ℕ∞) := by
  have hIJtop := sup_ne_top_of_ringKrullDim_quotient_eq_zero I J h0
  have hItop : I ≠ ⊤ := fun h => hIJtop (top_le_iff.mp (h ▸ le_sup_left))
  have hJtop : J ≠ ⊤ := fun h => hIJtop (top_le_iff.mp (h ▸ le_sup_right))
  have : Nontrivial (A ⧸ I) := Ideal.Quotient.nontrivial_iff.mpr hItop
  have : IsLocalRing (A ⧸ I) :=
    IsLocalRing.of_surjective' (Ideal.Quotient.mk I) Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective
  have : Nontrivial (A ⧸ J) := Ideal.Quotient.nontrivial_iff.mpr hJtop
  have : IsLocalRing (A ⧸ J) :=
    IsLocalRing.of_surjective' (Ideal.Quotient.mk J) Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective
  have hd1 : ringKrullDim (A ⧸ I) + (c : WithBot ℕ∞) = ringKrullDim A := by
    have h := ringKrullDim_add_length_eq_ringKrullDim_of_isRegular (List.ofFn f) hf
    rwa [Ideal.ofList_ofFn, ← hI, List.length_ofFn] at h
  obtain ⟨m, hm⟩ := exists_nat_ringKrullDim (A ⧸ I)
  obtain ⟨n, hn⟩ := exists_nat_ringKrullDim (A ⧸ J)
  obtain ⟨N, hN⟩ := exists_nat_ringKrullDim A
  rw [hm, hN] at hd1
  rw [hm, hn, hN] at hproper
  have hle := ringKrullDim_quotient_le_of_ringKrullDim_quotient_sup_eq_zero f I J hI h0
  rw [hn] at hle
  have e1 : m + c = N := by exact_mod_cast hd1
  have e2 : N ≤ m + n := by exact_mod_cast hproper
  have e3 : n ≤ c := by exact_mod_cast hle
  rw [hn, show n = c by omega]

/-! ### The multiplicity formula -/

/-- The quotient of `A ⧸ J` by the submodule generated by `I` is `A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)`. -/
public def quotSmulTopEquivQuotSup {A : Type u} [CommRing A] (I J : Ideal A) :
    ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ (I • (⊤ : Submodule A (A ⧸ J)))) ≃ₗ[A] A ⧸ (I ⊔ J) := by
  have hmap : Submodule.map (Submodule.mkQ (J : Submodule A A)) (I : Submodule A A)
      = I • (⊤ : Submodule A (A ⧸ J)) := by
    calc
      Submodule.map (Submodule.mkQ (J : Submodule A A)) (I : Submodule A A)
          = Submodule.map (Submodule.mkQ (J : Submodule A A)) (I • (⊤ : Submodule A A)) := by
            congr 1
            ext x
            simp
      _ = I • Submodule.map (Submodule.mkQ (J : Submodule A A)) (⊤ : Submodule A A) :=
            Submodule.map_smul'' I _ _
      _ = I • (⊤ : Submodule A (A ⧸ J)) := by simp [Submodule.range_mkQ]
  have hsup : (J : Submodule A A) ⊔ (I : Submodule A A) = ((I ⊔ J : Ideal A) : Submodule A A) :=
    sup_comm _ _
  exact ((Submodule.quotEquivOfEq _ _ hmap.symm).trans
      (Submodule.quotientQuotientEquivQuotientSup (J : Submodule A A) (I : Submodule A A))).trans
    (Submodule.quotEquivOfEq _ _ hsup)

/-- If `A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)` is zero dimensional, so is the support of `(A ⧸ J) ⧸ I • ⊤`. -/
private lemma supportDim_quotient_smul_top_eq_zero {A : Type u} [CommRing A] [IsNoetherianRing A]
    (I J : Ideal A) (h0 : ringKrullDim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0) :
    Module.supportDim A ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ (I • (⊤ : Submodule A (A ⧸ J)))) = 0 := by
  rw [Module.supportDim_eq_of_equiv (quotSmulTopEquivQuotSup I J),
    Module.supportDim_quotient_eq_ringKrullDim]
  exact h0

/-- **Stacks 0B04, in local form at the generic point.** Let `A` be a regular local ring, let `I`
be an ideal generated by a regular sequence `f₁, …, f_c`, and let `J` be an ideal with
`dim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0` and `dim A ≤ dim (A ⧸ I) + dim (A ⧸ J)`. Then the function
`t ↦ length_A ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ I ^ t (A ⧸ J))` is computed for `t ≫ 0` by a rational polynomial of
degree `c` with positive leading coefficient, and the Tor-formula intersection multiplicity of
the pair is `c !` times that leading coefficient.

Geometrically, for `A` the local ring of a nonsingular variety `X` at the generic point `ξ` of an
irreducible component `Z` of `V ∩ W`, with `I` and `J` the local ideals of two closed
subvarieties intersecting properly and `I` cut out by a regular sequence of length `c`, this says
that `e(X, V · W, Z)` is `c !` times the leading coefficient of the Hilbert polynomial
`t ↦ length_{𝒪_{X, ξ}} 𝒪_{W, ξ} / (f₁, …, f_c) ^ t`, and that this coefficient is positive. -/
@[stacks 0B04 "the multiplicity formula and the positivity of the leading coefficient, in local
form at the generic point"]
theorem localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_factorial_mul_leadingCoeff {A : Type u} [CommRing A]
    [IsRegularLocalRing A] {c : ℕ} (f : Fin c → A) (I J : Ideal A)
    (hf : RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular A (List.ofFn f))
    (hI : I = Ideal.span (Set.range f))
    (h0 : ringKrullDim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0)
    (hproper : ringKrullDim A ≤ ringKrullDim (A ⧸ I) + ringKrullDim (A ⧸ J)) :
    ∃ P : ℚ[X], I.ComputesGeneralizedHilbertPoly (A ⧸ J) P ∧
      P.degree = (c : WithBot ℕ) ∧ 0 < P.leadingCoeff ∧
      I.localIntersectionMultiplicity (A ⧸ J) = (c.factorial : ℚ) * P.leadingCoeff := by
  have hM0 := supportDim_quotient_smul_top_eq_zero I J h0
  have hMc : Module.supportDim A (A ⧸ J) = (c : WithBot ℕ∞) := by
    rw [Module.supportDim_quotient_eq_ringKrullDim]
    exact ringKrullDim_quotient_eq_of_intersectProperly f I J hf hI h0 hproper
  obtain ⟨P, hP⟩ := Ideal.exists_computesGeneralizedHilbertPoly I hM0
  have hdeg : P.degree = (c : WithBot ℕ) := by
    have h := Ideal.degree_eq_supportDim_of_computesGeneralizedHilbertPoly I hM0 hP
    rw [hMc] at h
    have hinj : Function.Injective (WithBot.map (Nat.cast : ℕ → ℕ∞)) :=
      WithBot.map_injective (fun a b hab => by exact_mod_cast hab)
    apply hinj
    rw [h]
    simp
  have hPne : P ≠ 0 := fun h => by simp [h] at hdeg
  have hnonneg : 0 ≤ P.leadingCoeff := by
    refine Stacks.HilbertFunction.PolynomialQuotient.leadingCoeff_nonneg_of_eventually_nonneg P ?_
    obtain ⟨N, hN⟩ := Filter.eventually_atTop.mp hP
    refine Filter.eventually_atTop.mpr ⟨(N : ℤ), fun k hk => ?_⟩
    obtain ⟨p, -, hp2⟩ := hN k.toNat (by omega)
    have hcast : ((k.toNat : ℕ) : ℚ) = (k : ℚ) := by
      have : (k.toNat : ℤ) = k := Int.toNat_of_nonneg (by omega)
      exact_mod_cast congrArg (fun z : ℤ => (z : ℚ)) this
    rw [← hcast, hp2]
    positivity
  refine ⟨P, hP, hdeg, hnonneg.lt_of_ne' (Polynomial.leadingCoeff_ne_zero.mpr hPne), ?_⟩
  rw [Ideal.localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_generalizedHilbertSamuelMultiplicity f I (A ⧸ J)
      hf hI hM0,
    Ideal.generalizedHilbertSamuelMultiplicity_eq_factorial_mul_coeff I (A ⧸ J) hP c,
    Polynomial.leadingCoeff, Polynomial.natDegree_eq_of_degree_eq_some hdeg]

/-! ### Vanishing when the intersection is deficient -/

/-- **The Tor-formula multiplicity vanishes when the intersection is deficient.** Let `A` be a
regular local ring, let `I` be an ideal generated by a regular sequence `f₁, …, f_c`, and let `J`
be an ideal with `dim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0` and `dim (A ⧸ J) < c`. Then the Tor-formula
intersection multiplicity of the pair is zero.

This is the exact companion of `localIntersectionMultiplicity_pos_of_isRegularSequence`, and the
two exhaust the possibilities: by `ringKrullDim_quotient_le_of_ringKrullDim_quotient_sup_eq_zero`
the dimension of `A ⧸ J` is always at most `c`, and it is exactly `c` precisely when the pair
intersects properly. In that case the generalized Hilbert polynomial has degree `c` and `c !`
times its `c`-th coefficient — the multiplicity — is its positive leading coefficient. Here the
dimension is strictly smaller, the polynomial has degree `dim (A ⧸ J) < c`, and that same `c`-th
coefficient is zero.

Geometrically, for `A = 𝒪_{X, ξ}` at the generic point `ξ` of an irreducible component `Z` of
`V ∩ W` on a nonsingular variety, this is the vanishing clause the dévissage of Tag 0B0W needs:
when `dim V + dim W` falls short of `dim Z + dim X` the cycle `[𝒪_V]_r · [𝒪_W]_s` is zero, and
the alternating sum of the `Tor` lengths at `ξ` must vanish with it. -/
theorem localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_zero_of_isRegularSequence {A : Type u} [CommRing A]
    [IsRegularLocalRing A] {c : ℕ} (f : Fin c → A) (I J : Ideal A)
    (hf : RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular A (List.ofFn f))
    (hI : I = Ideal.span (Set.range f))
    (h0 : ringKrullDim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0)
    (hdef : ringKrullDim (A ⧸ J) < (c : WithBot ℕ∞)) :
    I.localIntersectionMultiplicity (A ⧸ J) = 0 := by
  have hM0 := supportDim_quotient_smul_top_eq_zero I J h0
  obtain ⟨P, hP⟩ := Ideal.exists_computesGeneralizedHilbertPoly I hM0
  have hdegmap := Ideal.degree_eq_supportDim_of_computesGeneralizedHilbertPoly I hM0 hP
  rw [Module.supportDim_quotient_eq_ringKrullDim] at hdegmap
  have hlt : P.degree < (c : WithBot ℕ) := by
    cases hd : P.degree with
    | bot => simp
    | coe d =>
      rw [hd, WithBot.map_coe] at hdegmap
      rw [← hdegmap] at hdef
      have hdc : d < c := by exact_mod_cast hdef
      simpa [Nat.cast_withBot] using hdc
  rw [Ideal.localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_generalizedHilbertSamuelMultiplicity f I (A ⧸ J)
      hf hI hM0,
    Ideal.generalizedHilbertSamuelMultiplicity_eq_factorial_mul_coeff I (A ⧸ J) hP c,
    P.coeff_eq_zero_of_degree_lt hlt, mul_zero]

/-! ### The case of an effective Cartier divisor -/

/-- **Stacks 0B05.** In the situation of Tag 0B04, assume `c = 1`, so that `I = (f₁)` with `f₁` a
nonzerodivisor of `A`, and assume `A ⧸ J` is a domain. Then the Tor-formula intersection
multiplicity of the pair is the single length `length_A ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ f₁ (A ⧸ J))`.

Geometrically, for `A = 𝒪_{X, ξ}` at the generic point `ξ` of an irreducible component `Z` of
`V ∩ W` and `I`, `J` the local ideals of two closed subvarieties intersecting properly, `c = 1`
says that `V` is an effective Cartier divisor and `A ⧸ J = 𝒪_{W, ξ}` is a domain because `W` is
a variety; the conclusion is
`e(X, V · W, Z) = length_{𝒪_{X, ξ}}(𝒪_{W, ξ} / f₁ 𝒪_{W, ξ})`. The hypothesis
`dim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0` forces `f₁ ∉ J`, so `f₁` is a nonzerodivisor on the one-dimensional
domain `A ⧸ J` and `length_A ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ f₁ⁿ (A ⧸ J)) = n · length_A ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ f₁ (A ⧸ J))`. -/
@[stacks 0B05 "the multiplicity formula for an effective Cartier divisor, in local form at the \
generic point: for `I` generated by a single nonzerodivisor `f₁` of the regular local ring `A` \
and `A ⧸ J` a domain, the Tor-formula multiplicity of the pair is \
`length_A ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ f₁ (A ⧸ J))`. The hypotheses are those of Tag 0B04, transported to \
`A = 𝒪_{X, ξ}` exactly as there, with the extra hypothesis that `A ⧸ J` is a domain, which the \
source carries in the standing assumption that `W` is a closed subvariety and which the local \
form of Tag 0B04 does not need; without it the conclusion fails, for instance for \
`A = k[x, y]` at the origin with `I = (y)` and `J = (x², xy)`."]
theorem localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_length_quotient_smul_of_isDomain {A : Type u}
    [CommRing A] [IsRegularLocalRing A] (f : Fin 1 → A) (I J : Ideal A) [IsDomain (A ⧸ J)]
    (hf : RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular A (List.ofFn f))
    (hI : I = Ideal.span (Set.range f))
    (h0 : ringKrullDim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0)
    (hproper : ringKrullDim A ≤ ringKrullDim (A ⧸ I) + ringKrullDim (A ⧸ J)) :
    I.localIntersectionMultiplicity (A ⧸ J) =
      ((Module.length A ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ I • (⊤ : Submodule A (A ⧸ J)))).toNat : ℚ) := by
  have hM0 := supportDim_quotient_smul_top_eq_zero I J h0
  have hnt : Nontrivial ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ (I • (⊤ : Submodule A (A ⧸ J)))) :=
    (Module.supportDim_ne_bot_iff_nontrivial A _).mp (by rw [hM0]; norm_num)
  have hfin : Module.length A ((A ⧸ J) ⧸ (I • (⊤ : Submodule A (A ⧸ J)))) ≠ ⊤ :=
    ne_of_lt (((Ideal.idealOfDefinition_support_length_tfae I hnt).out 4 5).mp (le_of_eq hM0))
  have hspan : I = Ideal.span {f 0} := by
    rw [hI]
    congr 1
    exact Set.ext fun x => by simp [eq_comm]
  have hdimJ : ringKrullDim (A ⧸ J) = (1 : WithBot ℕ∞) := by
    simpa using ringKrullDim_quotient_eq_of_intersectProperly f I J hf hI h0 hproper
  have hf0J : f 0 ∉ J := by
    intro hmem
    have hle : I ≤ J := by
      rw [hspan]
      exact Ideal.span_le.mpr (by simpa using hmem)
    rw [sup_eq_right.mpr hle, hdimJ] at h0
    norm_num at h0
  have hne0 : (algebraMap A (A ⧸ J)) (f 0) ≠ 0 := by
    simpa [Ideal.Quotient.algebraMap_eq, Ideal.Quotient.eq_zero_iff_mem] using hf0J
  have hreg : IsSMulRegular (A ⧸ J) (f 0) :=
    fun x y hxy => mul_left_cancel₀ hne0 (by simpa only [Algebra.smul_def] using hxy)
  have hP := Ideal.computesGeneralizedHilbertPoly_of_eq_span_singleton I hspan hreg hfin
  rw [Ideal.localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_generalizedHilbertSamuelMultiplicity f I (A ⧸ J)
      hf hI hM0,
    Ideal.generalizedHilbertSamuelMultiplicity_eq_factorial_mul_coeff I (A ⧸ J) hP 1]
  simp

end Stacks.Intersection

end
